7.43.336.845 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"answered\",\n  \"answer\": \"Excretion is the process by which living organisms remove waste products and excess substances from their bodies. These wastes are generated as a result of various metabolic activities, such as respiration, digestion, and other cellular processes. The waste products include substances like carbon dioxide, water, nitrogenous compounds (like ammonia, urea, and uric acid), and excess salts. These substances can be harmful if not removed, so excretion is essential for maintaining the balance of the body's internal environment.\\n\\nThe process of urine formation involves several steps. First, the kidneys filter the blood through tiny structures called nephrons. In the glomerulus of the nephron, blood is filtered, and water, salts, and waste products are separated from the blood. This initial filtrate then passes through the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), where useful substances like glucose, amino acids, and some ions are reabsorbed back into the blood. Next, in the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and the loop of Henle, further reabsorption and secretion of ions occur. Finally, the remaining fluid, now called urine, is concentrated through the collecting duct and stored in the urinary bladder until it is excreted through the urethra.\",\n  \"selected_evidence_ids\": [\"E1\", \"E4\"],\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E1\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 87,\n      \"textbook_page\": 78\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E4\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 104,\n      \"textbook_page\": 95\n    }\n  ],\n  \"missing_information\": []\n}"}
