7.45.232.723 D slot launch_slot_: id  0 | task -1 | launching slot : {"id":0,"n_ctx":8192,"speculative":false,"is_processing":false,"id_task":686,"n_prompt_tokens":5399,"n_prompt_tokens_processed":4905,"n_prompt_tokens_cache":0,"params":{"seed":42,"temperature":0.0,"dynatemp_range":0.0,"dynatemp_exponent":1.0,"top_k":40,"top_p":1.0,"min_p":0.0,"top_n_sigma":-1.0,"xtc_probability":0.0,"xtc_threshold":0.10000000149011612,"typical_p":1.0,"repeat_last_n":64,"repeat_penalty":1.0,"presence_penalty":0.0,"frequency_penalty":0.0,"dry_multiplier":0.0,"dry_base":1.75,"dry_allowed_length":2,"dry_penalty_last_n":8192,"dry_sequence_breakers":["\n",":","\"","*"],"mirostat":0,"mirostat_tau":5.0,"mirostat_eta":0.10000000149011612,"stop":[],"max_tokens":384,"n_predict":384,"n_keep":0,"n_discard":0,"ignore_eos":false,"stream":true,"logit_bias":[],"n_probs":0,"min_keep":0,"grammar":"","grammar_lazy":false,"grammar_triggers":[],"preserved_tokens":[151657,151658,151667,151668],"chat_format":"peg-native","reasoning_format":"deepseek","reasoning_in_content":false,"generation_prompt":"<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","samplers":["penalties","dry","top_n_sigma","top_k","typ_p","top_p","min_p","xtc","temperature"],"speculative.types":"none","timings_per_token":false,"post_sampling_probs":false,"backend_sampling":false,"lora":[]},"next_token":[{"has_next_token":false,"has_new_line":false,"n_remain":15,"n_decoded":369}],"prompt":"<|im_start|>user\n/no_think\nUse only the supplied textbook evidence. Do not use outside knowledge. If it cannot support the question, return insufficient_evidence. Never invent page numbers. First select the relevant evidence IDs internally, then write the answer. Make the answer detailed and suitable for a Class 10 student.\nReturn only valid JSON with exactly this shape: {\"status\":\"answered|insufficient_evidence\",\"answer\":\"...\",\"selected_evidence_ids\":[\"E1\"],\"citations\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E1\",\"pdf_page\":1,\"textbook_page\":1}],\"missing_information\":[]}\n\nQUESTION:\nWhat is meant by excretion? Explain the process of formation of urine?\n\nTEXTBOOK EVIDENCE:\n[E1] PDF 87; textbook 78\nExcretion78\nThere is no factory which can manufacture a product without generating\nany waste.\nThis is true to our body which is a living cellular factory and for\nother organisms as well while performing various metabolic activities\nwastes are generated at regular intervals from the bodies of living\norganisms.\nWhen we think about this matter, we get many doubts.\nFor\nexample;\nx Where are the wastes produced?\nx How are they produced?\nx What are the substances present in them?\nx Does the composition of wastes vary in the same organism in different\nsituations?\nSuch thought provoking questions will lead to understand excretion\nprocess clearly.\nLet us discuss such kind of questions in this lesson.\nLiving organisms need energy for their survival and to perform\nmetabolic activities.\nThey are either anabolic activities or catabolic\nactivities.\nThese are collectively called as metabolic activities.\nAll the organisms\nuse different substances for metabolic activities.\nDifferent products are\ngenerated as a result of these metabolic activities.\nDiscuss and write the\ndifferent products generated during different metabolic activities in table-1\n\u0017Chapter\nExcretion\nTable-1\nLife processes Products\nPhotosynthesis\nRespiration\nDigestion\nExcretion79\nx What products do the organism take up for other activities?\nx What are the products which would cause harm to the body, if they\nare not removed?\nx What happens if harmful products are not removed from our body\nevery day?\nWe have already learnt that different kinds of materials are produced\nout of various metabolic activities.\nSome of these may be harmful for the\norganism are removed from their body or packed and stored in some other\nforms.\nThese are all wastes produced in the body of an organism.\nWe have already discussed how organisms get rid of gaseous wastes\ngenerated during photosynthesis or respiration.\nOther metabolic activities\ngenerate nitrogenous wastes that have to be removed along with salts,\nexcess water and several other materials.\nExcretion is the term coined for\nall the biological process involved in separation and removal of wastes or\nnon useful products from the body. (In latin ex means 'out', crenere means\n'shift'.)\nNow let us study how excretion takes place in human beings.\n4.1 Excretion in Human Beings\nA number of reactions take place during various metabolic activities.\nMany useful substances and energy are produced.\nAt the same time many\nother things happen such as, toxic wastes may be produced, water content\nmay increase, ionic balance (homeostasis) in the body may be disturbed.\nThe waste products include carbon dioxide, water, nitrogenous compounds\nlike ammonia, urea, uric acid, bile pigments, excess salts etc.\nThe most\npoisonous of all waste products of metabolism is ammonia.\nx Where are these waste materials produced?\nx How does the body manage them?\nx Is there a way to detect their presence in our body?\nExcretion80\nTable-2: DEPARTMENT OF BIOCHEMISTRY\nREPORT ON PLASMA/SERUM (BLOOD) ANALYSISS\nTEST NAME RESULT UNITS RANGE\nGLUCOSE - FASTING 82 mg/dl 60-100\nSODIUM 137 m.moles/L 135-145\nPOTASSIUM 4.10 m.moles/L 3.5-5.0\nCHLORIDES 101 m.moles/L 95-106\nUREA 29 mg/dl 15-40\nCREA TININE 2.8. mg/dl 0.6-1.5\nURIC ACID 7.50 mg/dl 3.0-5.0\nTOTAL CHOLESTEROL 221 mg/dl 150-200\nTRIGLYCERIDES 167 mg/dl 60-200\nCALCIUM 9.40 mg/dl 8.0-10.5\nPHOSPHORUS 4.50 mg/dl 3-4.5\nBILIRUBIN (TOTAL) 0.70 mg/dl 0.1-0.8\nTOTAL PROTEINS 7.20 g/dl 6.0-7.5\nALBUMIN 4.60 g/dl 3.0-5.0\nTable-3: DEPARTMENT OF BIOCHEMISTRY\nREPORT ON URINE ANALYSIS\nTEST/METHOD RESULT UNITS RANGE\n24 hrs.Protein 90 mg/day <100 mg\n24 hrs Creatinine 2.7 mg/day 1-2\n24 hrs.Calcium 305 mg/day Up to 200\n24hrs.Phosphorous 0.8 mg/day upto 1g\n24hrs.Uric acid 800 mg/day upto 600\n\n[E2] PDF 95; textbook 86\n4.3.2 Urinary bladder\nIt is a median, pear shaped and distensible sac.\nIt is situated in the\npelvic region on the ventral side of the rectum in the abdomen.\nIt stores\n300-800ml of urine temporarily which is brought by two ureters.\n4.3.3 Urethra\nIt is a tube that takes urine from urinary bladder to outside.\nThe opening\nof urinary bladder into urethra is guarded by a ring of muscles or sphincter.\nUrethra is 4 cm long in females and open in to vestibule where as in males\nit is about 20cm long.\nIts opening is separate in females but is in common\nwith the reproductive tract in males (urino-genital duct).\n4.4 Micturition\nThe urine is temporarily stored in the bladder.\nThere are two sets of\ncircular sphincter muscles in the bladder.\nWhen the bladder is filling up,\nboth these sets of muscles are constricted, so the exit is closed.\nHowever\nas the pressure of the urine increases the walls of the bladder are stretched\nand this triggers off an automatic reflex action which causes the upper\nsphincter to relax.\nBut the lower sphincter is under the control of brain.\nSo urination is controlled.\nControl of urination is not possessed by very\nyoung children but is gradually learned.\nUrinary bladder can store a maximum of 700-800 ml. of urine.\nUrge\nfor micturition occurs when urinary bladder is filled with 300 - 400 ml. of\nurine.\nThe stretched bladder stimulates nerve endings and sends signlas to\nthe brain.\nAt this time the urge becomes painful and leads to voluntary\nmicturition (release of urine from urinary bladder).\nTotal amount of urine\nexcreted per day is about 1.6-1.8 litres.\nIts quantity increases with larger\nintake of fluids like water, fruit juices and decreases with lesser intake.\nExcretion87\n4.5 Composition of urine\nUrine is light yellowish fluid.\nUrochrome formed by the breakdown\nof haemoglobin in the blood is responsible for the yellowish colour of\nurine.\nIt is a transparent fluid formed by urinary system.\nComposition of\nnormal urine varies considerably depending on several factors.\nFor instance\ntaking a protein rich diet will results the formation of more urea in the\nurine.\nThis is because the proteins get de-aminated in the liver with\nsubsequent formation of urea.\nEven sugar can appear in a normal person\nafter a heavy intake of carbohydrates (sugars).\nA large intake of liquids or\nwater - rich food increases the volume of water in the blood, hence more\noften urine is excreted.\nUrine contains 96% of water, 2.5% of organic substances (urea, uric\nacid, creatine, creatinine, water soluble vitamins, hormones, and oxalates\netc) and 1.5% of inorganic solutes (sodium, chloride, phosphate, sulphate,\nmagnesium, calcium, iodine etc).\nIt is acidic (pH=6.0)in the beginning\nbut gradually becomes alkaline due to breakdown of urea to form ammonia.\nx What happens if both kidneys fail completely?\nComplete and irreversible kidney failure is called End Stage Renal\nDisease (ESRD).\nIf kidneys stop working completely, our body is filled\nwith extra water and waste products.\nThis condition is called \"uremia.\"\nHands and feet may swell.\nYou feel tired and weak because blood is not\npurified properly.\nIs there any solution to this problem?\nLet us know about artificial kidneys.\nThink and discuss\nxDo all cells need excretion?\nxWhy are we advised to take sufficient water?\nxWhy do some children pass urine during sleep at night until 15\nor 16 years of age?\nExcretion88\n4.6 Dialysis Machine (Artificial kidney)\nKidneys are vital organs for survival.\nSeveral factors like infections, injury, very\nhigh blood pressure, very high blood sugar\nor restricted blood flow to kidneys leads to\naccumulation of poisonous wastes in the\n\n[E3] PDF 98; textbook 89\nbut carry out excretion as a secondary function.\nLungs: In respiratory process lungs remove carbon dioxide and water\nvapour.\nSkin: It consists of large number of sweat glands richly supplied with\nblood capillaries, from which they extract sweat and some metabolic\nwastes from blood.\nSince the skin sends out plenty of water and small\namount of salts in the form of sweat.\nIt serves as an accessory excretory\norgan.\nFig-8: Kidney transplantation\nExcretion90\nSebaceous glands in skin secrete sebum, which contains waxes, sterols,\nhydrocarbons and fatty acids.\nx Collect information on sebum from library books and prepare a news\nbulletin, display it on bulletin board.\nx People in cold countries get very less/no sweat.\nDue to this what\nchanges occur in their skin and in other excretory organs?\nLiver: The life span of RBCs is 120 days and they are destroyed in liver.\nIt produces bile pigments (bilirubin ,biliverdin and urochrome) which are\nmetabolic wastes of haemoglobin of dead R.B.Cs.\nUrochrome is\neliminated through urine.\nBiliverdin and bilirubin are excreted through\nbile along with cholesterol, steroid hormones, extra drug, vitamins alkaline\nsalts etc. throw urine.\nLiver is also plays a key role in urea formation.\nLarge intestine: Excess salts of calcium, magnesium and iron are\nseparated by epithelial cells of colon (large intestine) and are eliminated\nalong with the faeces.\nSmall amount of nitrogenous wastes are also eliminated through saliva\nand tears.\n4.9 Excretion in other organisms\nDifferent organisms use varied strategies in excretion.\nSpecific\nexcretory organs are absent in unicellular organisms.\nThese organisms\nremove waste products by simple diffusion from the body surface into the\nsurrounding water.\nFresh water organisms like Amoeba, Paramoecium\npossess osmoregulatory organelle called contractile vacuole.\nIt collects\nexcess water and waste from the cell and slowly moves in the cytoplasm,\nreaches the surface and bursts to release its content to outside.\nThe main\nexcretion takes place through body surface (Diffusion).\nMulticellular organisms possess different excretory organs for\nremoval of waste materials from the body.\nStructural and functional\ncomplexity of excretory organs increases from sponges to humans.\nExcretion91\nSponges and coelenterates do not have special excretory organs.\nWater\ntransportation occurs in each cell.\nExcretory organs are first seen in\nplatyhelmenthes.\nThey are called as flame cells.\nObserve the table - 4.\n4.10 Excretion in plants\nDo plants excrete like animals ?\nWe are amazed to answer such type of questions.\nYou are aware that a\nvariety of end products are formed during metabolism and these\nnitrogenous wastes are important.\nPlants does not have specific organs to\nexcrete these wastes.\nPlants break down the waste substances at much\nslower rate than the animals.\nHence the production and accumulation of\nwaste is also much slower in plants.\nGreen plants in darkness (in night\ntime) plants that do not possess chlorophyll produce carbon dioxide and\nwater as respiratory waste products.\nOxygen is generated during\nphotosynthesis as a product, that exits out side through stomata of leaves\nand lenticels of stem.\nx How do the plants manage or send out waste products from its body?\nPlants can get rid of excess water by a process like transpiration and\nguttation.\nWaste products may be stored in leaves, bark, and fruits.\nTable-4\nName of the phylum Excretory system/ organ\nProtozoa Simple diffusion from the body surface in to\nthe surrounding water\nPorifera and coelenterates Water bathes almost all the cells\nPlatyhelminthes Flame cells\nNematoda Renette cells\nAnnelids Nephridia\nArthropoda Green glands, Malpighian tubules\nMollusca Meta nephridia\nEchinodermata Water vascular system\nReptiles, Aves and Mammals Kidneys\nExcretion92\nWhen these riped parts fall off from the tree then waste products in\nthem are get rid off.\n\n[E4] PDF 104; textbook 95\ntubules or nephrons, which are structural and functional units of kidney.\nx A nephron comprises bowman’s capsule, glomerulus, proximal convoluted tubule\n(PCT), Henle’s loop, distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and collecting tubule.\nx Formation of urine involves four stages.\n1) Glomerular filteration, 2) Tubular\nReabsorption, 3) Tubular Secretion, 4) Formation of concentrated Urine.\nx Kidneys remove nitrogenous waste from our body, maintains water balance\n(osmoregulation), salt concentration, pH, and blood pressure in human body.\nx Dialysis machine is an artificial kidney which filters the blood to remove the\nmetabolic wastes from the body .\nx Kidney transplantation is a permanent solution to renal failure patients.\nKey words\nCreatinine, peritubular network, podocyte, afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole,\nglomerulus, Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT), Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT), loop of\nHenle, calyces, micturition, urochrome, dialyser, heamodialysis, anticoagulant, alkaloids,\nbiodiesel.\nWhat we have learnt?\nExcretion96\nx Different animals have different excretory organs eg: Amoeba-contractile vacuole\nplatyhelminthes- flame cells, annelida-nephridia, arthropoda-malpighian tubule,\nreptiles, birds and mammals-kidneys.\nx There are no special organs for excretion in plants.\nPlants store different waste\nmaterials in leaves, bark, roots, seeds which fall off from the plants after\nripening.\nx Plant metabolites are of two types i) primary metabolites eg: proteins,\ncarbohydrates and fats. ii) secondary metabolites eg: alkaloids, gums, tannins, latex\nand resins.\nThey have economical importance.\nx Excretion is the removal of material from living beings where as secretion is\nmovement of materials from one point to other.\n1.\nWhat is meant by excretion?\nExplain the process of formation of urine. (AS1)\n2.\nHow does excretion takes place in amoeba?(AS1)\n3.\nWhat are the different excretory organs in human body?\nWhat are the excretory\nmaterial generated by them?(AS1)\n4.\nDeepak said that ‘Nephrons are functional and structural units of kidneys’ How\nwill you support him?(AS1)\n5.\nHow plants manage the waste materials?(AS1)\n6.\nWhy do some people need dialysis?\nExplain the principle involved in it.(AS1)\n7.\nWhat is meant by osmoregulation?\nHow is it maintained in human body?(AS1)\n8.\nDo you find any relationship between circulatory system and excretory system?\nWhat are they?(AS1)\n9.\nGive reasons(AS1)\nA.\nAlways vasopressin is not secreted.\nB.\nUrine in beginning is acidic in nature later it become alkaline.\nC.\nDiameter of efferent arteriole is smaller than afferent arteriole.\nD.\nUrine is slightly thicker in summer than in winter.\n10.\nWrite differences(AS1)\nA.\nPCT and DCT B.\nKidney and artificial kidney\nC.\nExcretion and secretion D.\nPrimary metabolites and\nsecondary metabolites\n11.\nThere is a pair of bean-shaped organs ‘P’ in the human body towards the back,\njust above the waist.\nA waste product ‘Q’ formed by the decomposition of\nunused proteins in liver is brought into organ ‘P’ through blood by an artery ‘R’.\nThe numerous tiny filters ‘S’ present in organ ‘P’ filter the blood and sent it into\ncirculatory system through a vein ‘T’.\nThe waste substance ‘Q’ and other waste\nsalts and excess water form a yellowish liquid ‘U’ which goes from organ ‘P’\ninto a bag like structure ‘V’ through two tubes ‘W’.\nImprove your learning\nExcretion97\nThis liquid is then thrown out of the body through a opening ‘X’. (AS1)\n(a) What is organ P? (b) What is wastage 'Q'?.\n(c) Name artery R? (d) What is the name of vein 'T'?\n(e) What are tiny filters 'S' known as? (f) What is the name of the liquid 'U'?\n(g) What is the name of structure 'V'? (h) What is the name of the tubules 'W'?\n(i) What is the name of opening 'X'?\n12.\nThe organ ‘A ’ of a person has been damaged completely due to a poisonous waste material ‘B’ has\n\n[E5] PDF 105; textbook 96\nImprove your learning\nExcretion97\nThis liquid is then thrown out of the body through a opening ‘X’. (AS1)\n(a) What is organ P? (b) What is wastage 'Q'?.\n(c) Name artery R? (d) What is the name of vein 'T'?\n(e) What are tiny filters 'S' known as? (f) What is the name of the liquid 'U'?\n(g) What is the name of structure 'V'? (h) What is the name of the tubules 'W'?\n(i) What is the name of opening 'X'?\n12.\nThe organ ‘A ’ of a person has been damaged completely due to a poisonous waste material ‘B’ has\nstarted accumulated in his blood, making it dirty .\nIn order to save this person’s life, the blood from\nan artery of person’s arm is made to flow into long tubes made of substance ‘E’ which are kept in\ncoiled form in a tank containing solution ‘F’.\nThis solution contains three materials ‘G’, ‘H’ and ‘I’\nsimilar proportions to those in normal blood.\nAs the blood passes through long tubes of substance\n‘E’, most of the wastes present in it go into solution ‘F’.\nThe clean blood is then sent into circulatory\nsystem of the perosn through a vein (AS1)\n(a) What is organ 'A'? (b) Name the wastes substance B.\n(c) What are the names E and F? (d) What are G , H and I present in the solution?\n(e) What is the process described above known as?\n13.\nImagine what happens if waste materials are not sent out of the body from time to time?(AS2)\n14.\nTo keep your kidneys healthy for long period, what questions will you ask a nephrologist/urologist?\n(AS2)\n15.\nWhat are the gum yielding plants in your surroundings?\nWhat procedure you should follow to\ncollect gum from plants? (AS3)\n16.\nCollect the information about uses of different kinds of alkaloids, take help of library or internet.(AS4)\n17.\nDraw a neat labelled diagram of L.S of kidney. (AS5)\n18.\nDescribe the structure of nephron with the help of a daigram. (AS5)\n19.\nDraw a block diagram showing the path way of excretory system in human beings. (AS5)\n20.\nIf you want to explain the process of excretion in kidney, draw the diagram needed for it. (AS5)\n21.\nList out the things that makes you amazing in excretory system of human being. (AS6)\n22.\nY ou read about ‘Brain dead’ people in this chapter.\nWhat discussions would you like to have why\nyou think so? (AS6)\n23.\nWe people have very less awareness about organ donation, to motivate people write slogans\nabout organ donation? (AS7)\n24.\nAfter learning this chapter what habits you would like to change or follow for proper functioning of\nkidneys? (AS7)\n1.\nExcretory organs of earthworm ______________.\n2.\nThe dark brown coloured outer zone in T.S. of kidney is called _____________.\n3.\nThe process of maintenance of water balance and ion concentration with in the organism is\ncalled________.\n4.\nReabsorption of useful product takes place in ____________ part of nephron.\nFill in the blanks\nExcretion98\n5.\nGums and resins are the __________ products of the plants.\n6.\nBowman’s capsule and glomerulus together make ___________.\n7.\nThe alkaloid used for malaria treatment is ________________.\n8.\nThe principle involved in dialysis is __________________.\n9.\nRubber is produced from ______________ of Heavea braziliensis of rubber plants.\n10. ________________ performed first Kidney Transplantation.\n1.\nThe structural and functional unit of human kidney is called ( )\n(A) Neuron (B) nephron (C) nephridia (D) flame cell\n2.\nThe excretory organ in cockroach ( )\n(A) malpighian tubules (B) renette cells (C) flame cells (D) nephridia\n3.\nWhich of the following is the correct path taken by urine in our body? ( )<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","generated":""}
