12.49.564.183 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"answered\",\n  \"answer\": \"The acid-and-leaf experiment is used to model how the stomach is protected from its own acid. In this experiment, two similar green leaves are taken. One leaf is coated with petroleum jelly or grease, while the other is left free. A weak acid is then applied to both leaves. After some time, the leaf without the grease or petroleum jelly is affected by the acid, while the leaf coated with grease remains unaffected. This is because the grease acts like the mucus lining in the stomach, which protects the stomach walls from the strong acid. The mucus secreted by the stomach forms a thin lining that counters the action of the acid, similar to how petroleum jelly protects the leaf. This experiment demonstrates that the mucus lining in the stomach protects it from the strong acids it secretes, just as the grease protects the leaf from the acid.\",\n  \"selected_evidence_ids\": [\"E1\"],\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E1\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 174,\n      \"textbook_page\": 165\n    }\n  ],\n  \"missing_information\": []\n}"}
