26.08.768.581 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"answered\",\n  \"answer\": \"Mendeleev’s periodic table had several limitations. One major limitation was the presence of anomalous pairs of elements, where elements with higher atomic weights were placed before those with lower atomic weights, such as tellurium (Te) and iodine (I). Additionally, elements with dissimilar properties were placed in the same group, like cobalt (Co) and nickel (Ni), which have similar properties, while others like chlorine (Cl) and manganese (Mn) have different properties. Mendeleev’s table also did not leave room for new elements, and it was restricted to only 56 elements. Furthermore, the table was based on atomic weight, which led to some inconsistencies in the arrangement of elements. The modern periodic table addresses these limitations by arranging elements based on their atomic number rather than atomic weight. This arrangement ensures that elements with similar properties are grouped together and eliminates the anomalies caused by the atomic weight-based arrangement. The modern periodic table also accounts for the electronic configurations of elements, which determine their chemical and physical properties, providing a more accurate and comprehensive classification of elements.\",\n  \"selected_evidence_ids\": [\"E1\", \"E3\", \"E4\"],\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E1\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 147,\n      \"textbook_page\": 137\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E3\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 152,\n      \"textbook_page\": 142\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E4\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 153,\n      \"textbook_page\": 143\n    }\n  ],\n  \"missing_information\": []\n}"}
