27.52.991.963 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"answered\",\n  \"answer\": \"Ohm’s law states that the potential difference (V) across a conductor is directly proportional to the current (I) passing through it, provided the temperature remains constant. Mathematically, this is expressed as V = IR, where R is the resistance of the conductor. To verify Ohm’s law, an experiment can be conducted using a circuit with a battery, a resistor (like an iron spoke), an ammeter, and a voltmeter. The procedure involves connecting the battery to the resistor and measuring the current and potential difference for different numbers of cells (from one to five). For each case, the ratio V/I is calculated. If the ratio remains constant, it confirms Ohm’s law. A graph of V vs. I should be a straight line passing through the origin. This experiment also shows that materials like LEDs do not obey Ohm’s law, as their V/I ratio is not constant, resulting in a curved graph.\",\n  \"selected_evidence_ids\": [\"E1\"],\n  \"citations\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"E1\",\n      \"pdf_page\": 208,\n      \"textbook_page\": 198\n    }\n  ],\n  \"missing_information\": []\n}"}
